<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Standard of Ur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Standard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> is a decorated box from the Royal Cemetery at Ur, dating to the mid-third millennium BCE. Its panels show scenes of war and peace. It depicts the heavy infantry formations of Sumerian warfare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur.jpg" alt="Standard of Ur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denis Bourez from France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum,_London_(8747049029)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Standard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> is a Sumerian artefact of the third millennium BCE, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. It comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Ur in the Early Dynastic III period, from the ancient city of Ur in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p>It was found in tomb PG 779, one of the largest royal tombs in the Royal Cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, which is associated with the king Ur-Pabilsag. It lay beside the skeleton of a ritually sacrificed man who may have been its bearer, near a shell cylinder seal with the name e-zi. It is thought to have decorated the outside of a hollow wooden box inlaid with a mosaic of shell, red limestone and lapis lazuli.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The present form is a reconstruction of a best guess: a box 21.59 centimetres wide and 49.53 centimetres long, with end pieces shaped as truncated triangles. Mosaic panels cover each long side, in three registers, and they have been called War and Peace. In the War panel a figure often taken to be a king, though he lacks the usual marks of kingship, stands taller than the rest in the top register, with his head breaking the frame, before his bodyguard and a four-wheeled wagon drawn by equids, facing naked, bound prisoners with bleeding wounds.</p><p>Below, soldiers in leather cloaks and helmets give way to a battle scene, and four wagons shown walking, cantering, galloping and rearing carry drivers and warriors over fallen enemies. In the Peace panel the king sits on a carved stool at a banquet, with six seated participants holding cups, a lyrist and a possible singer, while the lower registers show figures leading animals or carrying goods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Sir Leonard Woolley</a>'s excavation of 1927 and 1928 found the Standard in the corner of a chamber of a tomb that robbers had plundered long before. A workman noticed a piece of shell inlay. The wooden frame and the bitumen that held the mosaic had decayed, and the weight of the soil had crushed it, so the excavators filled hollows with plaster or wax and lifted small sections under wax.</p><p>Woolley took it for a standard carried on a pole, which gave it its name, but this is now thought unlikely. Other suggestions are the soundbox of a musical instrument and a chest for funds. The two sides may form one narrative, a battle followed by a victory feast. It was consolidated with paraffin in 1928, which caused deterioration, and was dismantled and reassembled in 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Standard of Ur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sumerian warfare had relied heavily on close-order infantry, as depicted on the <b>Standard of Ur</b> and the Stele of the Vultures; Sargon's army appears to have made greater use of lightly equipped troops and archers, which allowed more flexible tactics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Among about 1,850 burials Woolley counted sixteen or seventeen royal tombs; the unlooted tomb of Queen Puabi, known from a cylinder seal, yielded the <b>Standard of Ur</b>, and the Lyres of Ur, bull-shaped instruments with eleven strings, came to light in 1928.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
